.TH OSPREY 3
.SH NAME
osprey \- resource containers, per-core scheduling and request rings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind '#O' /n/osprey

.B /n/osprey/ctl
.B /n/osprey/status
.B /n/osprey/reply
.B /n/osprey/arena
.B /n/osprey/pip
.B /n/osprey/snap
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The osprey device gives access to the portable Osprey policy layer:
resource containers with a CPU budget and a deadline, one independent
earliest-deadline-first run queue per core, bounded memory and network
accounting, a private namespace per container, and a pair of
single-producer/single-consumer request and reply rings.
.PP
The policy layer holds no state of the host kernel's: it has no
.IR Proc ,
no
.IR Mach ,
and no device registers.  This device is the adapter between it and the
rest of the kernel, and it serialises all of it under one lock.
.PP
The rings live in one contiguous physical span, published as the
.B osprey
physical segment, so a process may
.IR segattach (2)
it and submit requests and collect replies without entering the kernel at
all.  Only servicing a request still goes through the device.  The span is
divided into a slice per container;
.B status
reports the size of the span, the size of a slice, and each container's
own offset within it.
.PP
.B ctl
accepts the following commands.  A container is named by an arbitrary
non-zero number chosen by the writer.  Times and budgets are in the units
reported as
.B hz
by
.BR status .
.TP
.BI reset
Discard every container and empty every run queue, and let go of
everything they were holding, as
.B exit
does for one: channels, bound processes, service processes, pages,
interfaces and uarts.  Every
.IR pip (5)
session and stream goes too, since the containers their fids name are
about to be different containers with the same numbers.
.TP
.BI create " id budget period deadline"
Create a container with the given CPU budget, replenishment period and
relative deadline.
.TP
.BI mode " id " [ besteffort | strict ]
How the container is held to its CPU reservation.  A best-effort
container, which is the default, may take time beyond it when nothing
with time left wants the core, and that time is counted apart from the
reservation.  A strict one is stopped until its period refills.
.TP
.BI limit " id memory netbytes netpackets"
Set the bounded resource caps.
.B create
leaves them at zero, so a container refuses every allocation until they
are named.
.TP
.BI attach " id core"
Release the container's task and insert it into that core's run queue in
deadline order.
.TP
.BI detach " id"
Remove the task from every run queue.
.TP
.BI exit " id"
Remove the task and free the container.  What it was holding goes with
it: the channels it had open, the process bound to it, its service
process, the pages it had taken, and any interface or uart it had been
given, which are left as they were before it was given them.
.TP
.BI schedule " core"
Make the earliest-deadline runnable task on the core the core's current
task.  The task is not removed from the run queue.  The clock does this
on its own; the command is for driving the scheduler by hand.
.TP
.BI charge " core cycles"
Bill cycles to the core and to the current task's container.
.TP
.BI migrate " id core"
Move a container to another core's run queue and release its task there.
.TP
.BI bindproc " id pid"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI unbindproc " id"
.PD
Bill a running process to the container, or stop billing it.  From then
on the clock charges the container for the cycles whichever core is
running that process spends in it, and the budget stops the process when
it is spent.  A process that exits stops being billed on its own.
.TP
.BI memfree " id bytes"
Give back memory
.B alloc
charged.
.TP
.BI netcharge " id bytes packets"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI netfree " id bytes packets"
.PD
Charge or release the container's network byte and packet accounting
against the caps
.B limit
set.
.TP
.BI balance " "
Move one task from the busiest core to the least busy, if they differ by
more than one task.
.TP
.BI poke " id offset text"
Copy text and a terminating null into the container's data area.  A
process that has mapped the span writes there itself; this is for the
shell.
.TP
.BI submit " id op " [ arg " ...]"
Put a request on the container's request ring.
.I op
is one of
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR open ,
.BR close ,
.BR wait ,
.BR send ,
.BR recv ,
.BR stat ,
.BR bind ,
.BR alloc ,
.BR yield ,
or
.BR poll .
Up to three argument words follow.  If the request ring is full the write
blocks until the dispatcher makes room.
.TP
.BI service " " [ n ]
Service up to
.I n
queued requests, one from each of the caller's containers in turn, and
put a reply on the originating container's reply ring.  The default is
one.  Only the caller's, because a request is answered in the name space
of whoever asked for it to be: servicing somebody else's container would
resolve their names here and leave the channel in their hands.
.TP
.BI serve " id"
Give the container a service process of its own, running in the name
space of the process that asks.  From then on its requests are answered
without anyone writing
.BR service :
the process sleeps until a request arrives, and the clock wakes it, so a
request submitted through the mapped ring is answered without the caller
entering the kernel at all.  The service process goes away with the
container.
.TP
.BI wait " id"
Block until the container has a reply to collect.
.TP
.BI semup " id n"
Give the container's
.IR n th
semaphore.  This is what an interrupt does for the task that serves its
device, and what one task does for another waiting on it.
.TP
.BI grant " id user"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI revoke " id user"
.PD
Hand the right to a container to another, by name, or take it back.
Only whoever the container is already for may do either \- as with every
other command here that names a container: attaching it, limiting it,
binding a process to it, giving it a device, submitting to it,
checkpointing it, taking its pages or destroying it are all refused to
anyone it is not for.  The ones that name no container \-
.BR reset ,
.BR create ,
.BR schedule ,
.BR charge ,
.BR balance ,
.BR service ,
.BR key ,
.B pair
and
.B pipether
\- are the machine's rather than a container's.
.B flip
asks about the container the page is taken from and not the one it is
given to, since that is a gift.  Nothing here
knows anything about that name: it is enough that whoever comes with it
is called that, which is what makes it a delegation and not a lookup.
Grants are written into a checkpoint, so they cross to another machine
with the container.
.TP
.BI page " id " [ alloc | i ]
Take a page for the container, or give back the one it holds at that
place in its list.
.TP
.BI flip " id id i"
Hand the page at that place in the first container's list to the second.
A page is a segment of its own, named
.BR ospreypage N,
so what was written in it is there for the container it is handed to:
the page is mapped, not copied.
.B status
lists the pages a container holds, as offsets in the span.
.TP
.BI key " text " | off
The key this machine signs with and believes.  Only the host owner sets
it, and nothing reads it back.  Two machines holding the same key take
each other's word about who is asking and nobody else's; a machine with
no key takes every word it is given, which is what it did before there
were keys at all.
.TP
.BI pair " name " [ offer | listen ]
Agree a key with whoever is on the other end of that interface, rather
than have the same one typed into both machines.  One says
.BR listen ,
the other says
.BR offer ;
each picks a number it keeps and sends what the published group makes of
it, and both arrive at the same secret without it ever crossing the
wire.  What this does not do is say who is on the other end: whoever
answers first is whom the key was agreed with.  Both machines then say
the same
.B key
line in
.BR status ,
and somebody who can see both compares them.  That is the whole of the
authentication, and it is a person.
.TP
.BI pipether " name " [ on | off ]
Answer
.IR pip (5)
on the interface called
.I name
rather than only through
.BR #O/pip .
A conversation is taken on it carrying ethernet type
.BR 0x910A ;
a frame with a group in it is answered with a frame of replies to
whoever sent it, and a group that asked for a stream has its frames sent
at the rate it asked for.  There is no connection: each group says which
session it belongs to.
.B off
gives the interface back and waits for the processes answering on it to
finish, so that
.B off
followed by
.B on
works: while one of them still holds the conversation, nothing else can
take that frame type.
.TP
.BI output " id off len"
Put work on the container's output queue: a place in its data area and
how much of it.  See
.B "A DRIVER SERVED BY A TASK"
below.
.TP
.BI delegate " id " [ server | off ]
Have another container answer the requests this one makes that the kernel
does not implement.  The request appears on the server's own request
ring, carrying the container and the sequence it came from, and whatever
the server leaves on its reply ring is routed back to them.  The server
is an ordinary process with the span mapped: nothing of the service is in
the kernel but the routing.
.TP
.BI ether " id name " [ "n" [ t ] | off ]
Give the container the ethernet interface called
.I name
-- \fBether0\fP -- with each packet giving the container's
.IR n th
semaphore.  A
.I t
after it names a second semaphore, which is given when the card has
finished with a transmit buffer, so that a container with every buffer
in flight can wait rather than offer one and be refused.  A packet that
arrives for a claimed interface is charged
against the container's network caps and queued for it whole, without
being copied and without reaching the rest of the network stack.  A
container that is over its caps, or has no room, refuses the packet and
the driver disposes of it as it would have.
.TP
.BI uart " id name " [ n | off ]
Give the container the uart called
.I name
-- \fBeia1\fP, or the uart's own name -- to serve, with its interrupt
giving the container's
.IR n th
semaphore.  From then on the interrupt does no more than that and masks
the device, and a task of the container's does the work the handler would
have done and lets the device interrupt again.  The task is a kernel
task, so neither its wait nor its work takes a trap.  The console's uart
is refused: losing it would take the machine with it.
.TP
.BI tick " id " [ n | off ]
Have the clock interrupt give the container's
.IR n th
semaphore on every tick of the core it is attached to, or stop.  This is
a device interrupt handing work to the task that serves it, with the one
interrupt this driver owns standing in for a device it does not have: the
interrupt gives the semaphore and wakes whoever waits on it, and does no
more, while the task runs in the scheduler like any other.
.TP
.BI select " id offset narms"
Wait on the arms the container has left in its data area at
.I offset
and perform the first that is ready.  Which arm that was is left in the
container's map, where the caller reads it without asking again.  The
write returns when an arm has been performed; arms that name something
the container does not have are refused rather than waited on.
.TP
.BI checkpoint " id"
Record the container's state where the kernel can put it back, without
writing it anywhere.
.TP
.BI restore " id"
Put that back.  For a checkpoint that leaves the kernel, read and write
.BR snap .
.TP
.BI bind " id local global"
Add a private name to the container's namespace.  This is the same table
the
.B bind
request writes and the same one every
.B open
is resolved through, and it is written into the container's checkpoint,
so the names go with it to another machine.  A name is at most
thirty-one bytes and a container may hold eight; one that does not fit
is refused rather than cut short, since what is in this table decides
what an
.B open
opens and half a name is not a smaller answer but a different one.
.TP
.BI unbind " id local"
Remove one.  A name is at most 31 characters and a container holds at
most eight; binding a local name that is already there replaces what it
refers to.  The table is a mapping the container keeps, not a mount: the
kernel does not resolve requests through it yet.
.SH SCHEDULING
.PP
Each core has its own run queue, its own lock and its own clock.  On every
tick the machine cycles since the last tick on that core are billed to the
container of the process the core is running, if one is bound to it, and
otherwise to the container the core's own run queue names.  A container that exhausts its CPU budget
before its period is out leaves the run queue, so the containers behind it
get their turn, and returns when its period refills.  The core then picks
the earliest-deadline runnable task again.  Only the lock of the core
being ticked is taken; moving a task between cores takes them all.  A
process may be run by a core other than the one its container is attached
to, so billing follows the process while the decision to leave or rejoin
a run queue belongs to the core that owns it.
.PP
A budget is a reservation.  Time within it is the container's to take;
time beyond it is best effort, counted apart from the reservation in the
container's
.B besteffort
total, and taken only when nothing with reservation left wants the core.
A process whose container is past its reservation drops to the lowest
fixed scheduling priority until the period refills.
.PP
A container put in
.B strict
mode is instead held to its reservation: its process is stopped when the
reservation is spent and started again when the period refills.  The
control word a debugger uses to stop a process is examined on the way out
of every trap, so it stops at the end of the clock interrupt that spends
the last of it.  Only processes stopped this way are started again this
way, and unbinding a container, or its exit, releases the one it held.
.PP
Reading
.B status
reports the clock, the tick rate, and one line per core and per
container.  A core's line gives the number of runnable tasks, the cycles
charged to it, the number of context switches, the current task and the
run queue in deadline order.  A container's line gives its core, state,
CPU budget and use, period, absolute deadline, best-effort time, periods
elapsed, mode, memory and network use against their caps, and the
occupancy of its three rings.
A last
.B key
line says what key this machine holds \- not the key, what SHA1 makes of
it \- or
.B none
if it holds none.  Two machines that agreed a key say the same thing
there; see
.B TICKETS
below.
.PP
Reading
.B reply
drains the reply ring of every container the reader may have \- reading
is taking, so it takes only from those \- and returns one line per
reply:
the container, the operation, the reply status
.RB ( pending ,
.BR done ,
.BR error ,
.BR blocked
or
.BR retry )
and three result words.  A ring has no stable offset, so only a read at
the start of the file takes anything.
.PP
.B arena
is the shared span itself at its own byte offsets, so a ring or a data
area can be examined without mapping it \- but only the parts of it the
caller may have.  A slice is its container's and a page is whichever
container holds it, so reading or writing across a container that is
somebody else's is refused, as attaching its segment would be.  A page
nobody holds is nobody's.
.PP
.B bench
says what a request costs; see
.B "WHAT IT COSTS"
below.
.PP
.B snap
is every container as text: its identity, the core it is on, whether it
was running, its budget, period and deadline, what it has used, its caps,
its version, whatever its rings are holding and its private names.
Reading it is
taking a checkpoint, and writing one back is a restart -- a container the
checkpoint names that is not there is made, one that is there is set to
what the checkpoint says, and one that was running when it was written
out is running again.  A checkpoint may make a container that is not
here; it may not write over one that is and is not the writer's, since a
checkpoint says who a container is for and writing one would otherwise
be a way of taking any container going.  A message a ring is holding is written out with
it, one line each, and put back on the same ring when the checkpoint is
read; a ring that is empty writes nothing, which is the usual case.  A
container with anything in a ring writes its data area out as well, in
lines of thirty-two bytes and only the lines that hold something, since
that is where the buffers those messages name are.  The packets, the
pages and the channels the kernel holds are not in it: what is written
is what a container is and what it is in the middle of, not everything
it happens to be holding.
.PP
A checkpoint is text and goes wherever text goes.
.I mig
carries one to another machine in an ethernet frame of its own type and
reads it back into the kernel there, destroying what it sent so that the
container is afterwards in one place and not two.  It destroys it only
when the other end has said the checkpoint arrived: a checkpoint that is
refused there \- signed under a key that machine does not hold, say \-
would otherwise leave the container gone from one machine and never
there on the other.
.PP
.B pip
speaks the session protocol described in
.IR pip (5),
in which a container's settings and accounting are named attributes, a
session outlives the connection carrying it, and as many transactions as
will fit are one round trip.
.SH REQUESTS
.PP
.BR yield ,
.B poll
and
.B alloc
are settled by the portable layer:
.B poll
reports the occupancy of the request and reply rings and
.B alloc
charges the container's memory budget, refusing the request when the cap
set by
.B limit
is reached.
.PP
.BR open ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.B stat
and
.B close
are served against real kernel channels, in the namespace of whichever
process drove the dispatcher.
.B open
takes the offset of a null-terminated path in the container's data area
and an open mode, and replies with a handle.  The path is resolved in the
name space of the container's service process, or of whoever wrote
.B service
when it has none.
.BR read ,
.B write
and
.B stat
take a handle, an offset into the data area, and a byte count, and reply
with the count transferred.
.B close
takes a handle.
.PP
.B recv
takes the next packet an interface has given the container and replies
with where it is in the container's slice and how long it is.  The card
wrote it there; nothing copies it.  The buffer belongs to the container
until it asks for the next packet, which is when the one before goes back
to the interface.  The container's network accounting is credited for the
packet as it is taken.
.PP
.B exec
starts a program in a container of its own.  It takes the offset of a
null-terminated name in the container's data area; the name is resolved
by the container's server, which lays the program out and supplies its
pages as it runs and answers its system calls, so a container with no
server has nothing to run it and is refused.  The reply is the new
container's identity.
.B wait
is answered when a container this one started exits, with its exit
status; if one has already exited it is answered at once, and a
container that has started nothing is refused.  The remaining
operations are refused.
.SH THE SHARED SPAN
.PP
Each container's slice is a segment of its own, named
.BR osprey N
and reported with its size by
.BR status ,
so that attaching one gets that container's memory and nobody else's.
A name is not enough to have it.  A container is for whoever made it,
and for whoever they have handed it to with
.BR grant ,
wherever those are running; and for the process bound to it by
.B bindproc
and the processes that one started, which is how a container reaches its
own memory before anyone has been named.  A page is for whichever
container holds it, so the right to a page goes with the page when it is
handed over.  A container that nobody owns and nothing is bound to is
nobody's.
.PP
.B status
reports who a container is for, and who it has been handed to.
.IP
.nf
segattach(0, "osprey0", 0, slicesize)
.fi
.PP
The map is written by the kernel and read by whoever has the slice.  It
is in memory the container may write, so the kernel puts it back from
what it knows before every use of it: what a container writes there is
its own view and nothing more.  The two ring indices are the exception,
since moving them is how a ring is worked; a ring whose indices say it
holds more than it can hold is left alone rather than served, and a
.B submit
against one is refused rather than waiting for room that will not come.
.PP
At the start of it a process findsAt the start of it a process finds
.IP
.nf
struct Ospreymap {
	uvlong	magic;		/* 0x4f53505259 */
	uvlong	id;
	uvlong	size;		/* messages in each ring */
	uvlong	msgsize;	/* bytes in one message */
	uvlong	requestqueue;	/* the rest are byte offsets */
	uvlong	replyqueue;	/* from the start of the slice */
	uvlong	outputqueue;	/* what a driver task takes work from */
	uvlong	request;
	uvlong	reply;
	uvlong	output;
	uvlong	data;
	uvlong	datalen;
	uvlong	sem;		/* the semaphores */
	uvlong	nsem;
	uvlong	selected;	/* the arm the last select performed */
};
.fi
.PP
Each ring head is
.IP
.nf
struct Queue {
	vlong	message;	/* bytes from here to the messages */
	uvlong	size;
	uvlong	put;
	uvlong	get;
};
.fi
.PP
and a message is
.IP
.nf
struct Message {
	uvlong	op;
	uvlong	sequence;
	uvlong	container;
	uvlong	arg[5];		/* arg[0] status, arg[1] flags */
};
.fi
.PP
To submit, write the message at index
.B put
modulo
.B size
of the request array and then increment
.BR put ;
to collect, read the reply array at
.B get
modulo
.B size
when
.B put
differs from
.B get
and then increment
.BR get .
The message must be stored before the index that publishes it, and the
index read before the message it refers to; the kernel side uses
.IR coherence (10.2)
for that, and a machine that reorders stores needs the same on this side.
.PP
Nothing in the span is a pointer, which is what lets the same slice be
mapped at more than one address \- the talk asks for exactly that.
.B "ring Isegment sizeP twice"
attaches one a second time, checks that the two are the same memory by
writing through one and reading through the other, and then works the
ring through the second alone: the map is read there, the ring heads are
found from it there, and the request is put there.  In the check the two
mappings come out sixty thousand bytes apart and the request is answered
.BR "poll done" .
.SS The span as a library
.PP
.B /os/osprey/libosprey9
is all of the above as C: attach a slice and check it, put a request on
the ring and take its answer, build the arms and wait on them, and the
file operations written the way a program would rather write them.
.I p9srv
and
.I netsrv
are built on it.  What matters about it is not the convenience: the map,
the ring head, the message, the arm and the semaphore had a copy in each
of those programs, and a field added to the map in the kernel had to be
added by hand to each copy or that program would read the wrong bytes,
which is a bug nothing catches.  The library includes the kernel's own
declarations \- 
.B /os/osprey/port/ospreyabi.h
for the map and the arm,
.B /os/osprey/policy
for the message, the ring head and the semaphore \- installed into
.B /Osprey/include/osprey
from the one place each is written.
.PP
It is not a library operating system in the sense the plan means.  A
Plan 9 kernel is still underneath, answering what the policy layer does
not; this is that kernel's request ABI made into a library, so that a
program need not work out the span's shape for itself.
A double did not work in these programs, and the reason was every
loader in the tree.
.I Ieee
holds a double as two longs, low word and high, which are thirty-two bit
values in the object file whatever the host's long is; every loader laid
the eight bytes of a double out by casting that struct to bytes, which
on a host with a sixty-four bit long copies the low word and the four
zero bytes above it and never the high word.  So 1.5, whose low word is
zero, linked as eight zero bytes, and a program that set a variable to
1.5 read back sixteen zero bits while the compiler's object held the
right value.  All nine loaders lay the two words out as a pair now, and
.I fpbits
prints what a double comes to once it has been through the compiler,
the loader and the kernel, as bits rather than a value:
.B "1.5 3ff8000000000000" ,
.B "3.0 4008000000000000" ,
.BR "compares yes" .
.PP
The compiler had a fault of its own that only a running machine could
show, and this one showed it.  fdlibm scales a result by adding to the
exponent field of a local double through a pointer, and
.I 6c
kept the double in a register across that write and returned it
unscaled:
.I fpbits
prints
.B scale(1.0, 0.5, 1)
and got
.B 3ff8000000000000
\- 1.5, where 3.0 is right.  Another session had found the same in
.I jc
and
.I tc ,
fixed them, and left
.I 6c
alone for want of a board that boots with it; its fix is in
.I 6c
now, the listing stores the double before the half-write and reloads it
after, and the line reads
.BR "scale 4008000000000000" .
.PP
.I ring
is deliberately not built on it: it exists to show the ring unmediated,
and would show nothing if it used something else to work it.
.I o9cat
is the other way about: it uses nothing else, and exists because
.I p9srv
answers with ordinary system calls and
.I netsrv
only ever submits, so neither of them ever calls the library's own
.B open
or
.BR read .
It reads a file through the ring and says whether the arm it built
before opening is still there afterwards, which is what the data area's
layout has to guarantee and did not at first.
.SS How big a slice is
.PP
Five of the numbers above are read from
.B plan9.ini
before the arena is made, since they do nothing but lay out a slice:
.TP
.B *ospreyqueue
Messages in each of the three rings; 16.
.TP
.B *ospreydata
Bytes of data area, names and page table together; 4096.
.TP
.B *ospreysem
Semaphores; 8.
.TP
.B *ospreypktbuf
Receive buffers the container lends an interface; 16.  A card's receive
ring is deeper than that \- igbe's is 256 \- and what the container has
not lent, the driver fills with its own and copies out of.
.TP
.B *ospreytxbuf
Transmit buffers it fills itself; 8, and at most 64, because
.B txavail
is one word of bits.
.PP
The whole arena is one allocation of a slice times sixteen, so raising
one of these raises what the kernel asks for at boot.  A value smaller
than the compiled-in one is refused and said so on the console rather
than obeyed: what is in the tree is already a demonstration's size and
taking memory away from it breaks a check rather than a container.  So
is one that would make the whole arena more than sixty-four megabytes,
and that ceiling is the reason the refusal is worth anything:
.I xspanalloc
panics rather than returning nothing, so a number that asked for more
than the machine has would stop the kernel at
.I devinit
and read as a broken kernel rather than as a line of
.B plan9.ini
that should not have been written.  The
numbers not here \- containers, cores, packets a container may hold,
private names, pages, arms to a
.B select
\- size arrays that are compiled in, and are in the enum at the head of
.BR /os/osprey/adapter/ospreyrt.c .
.SH A DRIVER SERVED BY A TASK
.PP
.B uart
hands a uart to a container.  From then on its interrupt handler does
nothing but silence the source and give the container's
.IR n th
semaphore, and a kernel task of the container's does the work \- so no
trap is taken for the wait or for the work, and the driver is scheduled
like everything else the container does.
.PP
The task waits on three things at once, which is the shape the
presentation gives a driver: the control messages sent to it on the
request ring, the semaphore its interrupt gives, and the
.I "output queue"
it takes work from.  A message on the output queue is a place in the
container's own data area and how much of it, so whoever has the slice
mapped fills a buffer and says where it is; nothing is copied.  The task
writes as much of it as the transmitter will take right now, and while
any of it is still going out it turns the output arm off in place \-
.B MSnoop
\- which is what
.B select
is described doing with a full fifo.  A control message is answered on
the reply ring:
.B open
sets the line speed and
.B close
gives the uart back.
.PP
.B output
puts work on that queue from the outside, for a caller that has not
mapped the slice.  A container's own process needs neither it nor a
trap: the queue is in memory it has mapped.
.SH SERVICES OUTSIDE THE KERNEL
.PP
An operation the kernel refuses is not the end of it.  A container with a
server has the request put on that server's request ring instead of being
answered, and the process serving it takes the request with a
.B MSget
arm and answers it with a
.B MSput
arm, naming the same message.  Answering is putting the message back with
a status in it: the container and sequence it arrived with are still
there, and are what the reply is routed by.
.PP
A container that serves others is passed over when requests are serviced,
because what is on its ring is there for its process; and it should not
also be asking, since its reply ring is where its answers go.
.PP
The server is asked first, not last.  A request that is not one of the
three the policy layer answers by itself \-
.BR yield ,
.B poll
and
.B alloc
\- is offered to the container's server before the kernel's own
personality is consulted, and only a container that has named no server
reaches
.BR ospreykernop() .
A container that has named one has
.BR open ,
.BR read ,
.B write
and
.B close
answered entirely outside the kernel, in the server's name space, with
the kernel doing nothing but carrying the message.  If the server has
named one but its ring is full the request is answered
.B retry
rather than falling through, so the personality a container replaced
does not answer for it whenever its server is busy.
.PP
.I p9srv
is such a server: an ordinary user program that maps its own slice,
waits on its request ring, performs each
.BR open ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR stat ,
.B close
with the ordinary system calls, and puts the answer back.  It refuses
everything else rather than falling back into the kernel.
.B bind
is not among them: a name space is not a personality, and the policy
layer answers it in
.IR dispatch ()
before anything reaches a server.  What the server does with the table
is read it: a name a container asks to open is looked up in that
container's own names first, and what comes back is opened in the
server's process.
.PP
.I p9srv
is started as
.IP
.B "p9srv segment size [nslice]"
.LP
where the segment is the server's own container slice and the size is
that slice's.
.PP
A request names its arguments as offsets into the
.I asking
container's data area, so the server maps that container's slice as
well.  It may: a container that has delegated to a server has thereby
given that server the right to reach the memory its own requests point
into, and that is now what the span's permission check says, one step
and not a chain.  Which slice belongs to which container the server asks
.B status
for rather than reading it out of the maps, since a container may write
its own map and could otherwise claim to be one of the others.  The
offsets within a slice it takes from its own map, which nobody else may
write, and applies to the client; a client that lied about
.B datalen
would otherwise have its server write a read past the end of the slice.
.SH SELECT
.PP
A task waits on several things at once by offering an array of arms.  An
arm names a ring to take a message from or add one to, or a semaphore to
take:
.IP
.nf
struct Ospreyarm {
	uvlong	op;
	uvlong	obj;	/* ring 0 or 1, or a semaphore */
	uvlong	buf;	/* the message, offset in the data area */
};
.fi
.PP
with
.I op
one of
.B MSlast
to end the array,
.B MSnoop
for an arm that is turned off,
.B MSget
and
.B MSput
for a ring, and
.B MSdown
for a semaphore.  An arm is turned off in place rather than the array
being built again: a driver with no room in its output fifo sets the arm
that would read its output queue to
.B MSnoop
and leaves the rest alone.  The arms live in the container's data area,
and every offset in them is measured from the start of that area, as
elsewhere.
.PP
Whether an arm is ready is visible to anyone who has mapped the span, so
a task can look before it asks.  What it may then do for itself depends
on the arm.  A container is the consumer of its own reply ring, so it may
take a reply by reading the message and stepping the get index, with no
trap at all.  A semaphore is shared with whoever gives it, so taking one
is the kernel's to do, and that is what the
.B select
command is for.
.PP
A semaphore counts, and reports how many times it has been given and
taken;
.B status
lists the counts of a container's semaphores on a line of their own.
Giving one is the only operation an interrupt performs, so semaphores are
held under a lock of their own rather than the one that serves the rest
of the device, which a handler could not take.
.SH GRAPHICS AND INPUT
.PP
The display and its input are exposed the way a driver in general is:
the kernel hands a user program the hardware it needs by name, and
does no drawing, no compositing and no PS/2 decoding of its own.
.PP
UEFI hands the kernel a linear frame buffer, and it is published at
.I ospreyinit
as the
.B framebuffer
physical segment, on every configuration \- the microkernel path and
the full 9front personality both map the same segment the same way.
A program that
.B segattach s
it and writes pixels is a complete driver;
.I os/osprey/cmd/fb.c
is the minimal one, and draws a gradient, a block and two bars with no
kernel graphics stack anywhere in the path.
.PP
.I draw
is the user-level draw service, built on
.IR os/osprey/cmd/draw/devdraw.c ,
a reuse of the kernel's own
.IR os/osprey/port/devdraw.c ,
whole, behind a small kernel-compat shim
.RI ( kshim.c ,
.IR kshim.h )
and a
.I lib9p
front end that turns each 9P request into the device's own
.B Chan
entry points, with
.I attachscreen
handed the exposed
.B framebuffer
segment as the screen's backing store and
.I softscreen
zero, so compositing lands on the frame buffer directly.  Mounted at
.BR /dev ,
it makes
.BR /dev/draw ,
which a real
.I libdraw
client reads and writes exactly as it would any Plan 9 draw device.
.I os/osprey/cmd/draw/devdraw.c
speaks the current draw protocol; reconciling it needed the
.B Warp
matrix as a struct rather than an array and stopped there \- the
affine-transform command has a newer wire form
.I rio
does not use, and it refuses rather than pretending to answer it.
.PP
The mouse and keyboard are each a page the kernel fills and a program
that reads it, nothing more.
.IR devosprey.c 's
.I ospreymouseexpose
and the keyboard's equivalent publish the PS/2 byte streams as the
.B mousering
and
.B kbdring
physical segments; the IRQ12 and IRQ1 handlers do nothing but copy a
byte into the ring and arm the next interrupt.
.I os/osprey/cmd/draw/mouse.c
segattaches its page, decodes the three-byte packets into a position
and a button mask, and serves
.B /dev/mouse
in the format
.I libdraw
reads;
.I os/osprey/cmd/draw/kbd.c
does the same for scancodes, tracks shift and control, translates
through a keymap into runes, and serves
.BR /dev/kbd .
Neither driver executes
.B in
or
.B out
\- both only read a page the kernel already filled.  The cursor is
drawn the same way anything else is: the draw server forks a process
that reads
.B mousering
and composites an arrow onto the frame buffer, under a lock shared
with the draw path so the two never collide on one frame.
.PP
With
.BR /dev/draw ,
.B /dev/mouse
and
.B /dev/kbd
all served from user space,
.I rio
runs against them unmodified: it paints its desktop on the exposed
frame buffer, a right-click pops its window menu, sweeping a new
window spawns
.I rc
in it, and a line typed at the keyboard reaches the focused window and
is answered by whatever runs there.  This is a full window system,
running interactively, with no kernel graphics, no kernel mouse and no
kernel keyboard \- three ordinary user programs above the microkernel
answer for all of it, the exact split the driver-as-a-task shape above
draws for any device.
.PP
What is not built this way: the second cut described under
.B "A DRIVER SERVED BY A TASK"
above, moving
.IR cons ,
.I uart
and
.I rtc
themselves out of the kernel into tasks of this same shape, is not yet
done for the console and the real-time clock \- only the uart has it.
And this section describes the full 9front personality's path to the
display; a container running under the minimal library OS
.RI ( xup64 )
does not arm the mouse or keyboard interrupts at the same time as the
full personality, so the two are not run together.
.SH EXAMPLE
Order three containers by deadline and bill the one that runs:
.IP
.nf
bind '#O' /n/osprey
echo create 10 1000000 100000000 90000000 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo create 20 1000000 100000000 30000000 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo create 30 1000000 100000000 60000000 >/n/osprey/ctl
for(i in 10 20 30)
	echo attach $i 0 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo schedule 0 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo charge 0 500000 >/n/osprey/ctl
cat /n/osprey/status
.fi
.PP
Charge a container's memory budget until it is refused:
.IP
.nf
echo limit 10 8192 65536 64 >/n/osprey/ctl
for(i in 1 2 3)
	echo submit 10 alloc 4096 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo service 4 >/n/osprey/ctl
cat /n/osprey/reply
.fi
.PP
Wait on a reply or on a semaphore, and let something else provide one:
.IP
.nf
echo create 5 40000000 800000000 200000000 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo attach 5 0 >/n/osprey/ctl
ring osprey0 40960 wait 1 &
echo semup 5 1 >/n/osprey/ctl
.fi
.PP
Read a file through the ring.  The data offset comes from the container's
line in
.BR status ;
here it is 2240, so the sixty-fourth byte of the data area is at 2304 in
the span:
.IP
.nf
echo poke 10 0 /dev/osversion >/n/osprey/ctl
echo submit 10 open 0 0 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo submit 10 read 0 64 32 >/n/osprey/ctl
echo service 4 >/n/osprey/ctl
cat /n/osprey/reply
dd -if /n/osprey/arena -bs 1 -iseek 2304 -count 4
.fi
