.SH CHECKING
.PP
.B /scripts/osprey-check.py
boots one kernel on four cores and asks it for everything a single
machine can be made to show: a request submitted and collected through
the mapped ring with nobody writing
.BR service ,
a select woken by a semaphore on the arm it was given, a page written by
one container and read by another after it is handed over, a private
name, a request still in flight and the buffer it names carried across a
checkpoint, an attribute written over
.IR pip (5)
that nobody else sees until the fid that wrote it is closed, an
operation the kernel refuses put on another container's ring on another
core and answered there, a slice mapped a second time somewhere else and
worked through the second mapping, a slice reached by a server of
another user because the container delegated to it and refused to the
same server for a container that did not, what a round trip costs with a
trap and without one, a
uart driven by a task whose bytes are watched leaving the second serial
port, sixteen hundred requests from eight processes at once with none
lost, the pages a container held coming back when it exits, eight idle
service processes costing nothing at all, a container held to a
five-per-cent reservation while an identical process beside it is not,
the wire
.IR pip (5)
is spoken on given back when it is turned off, a container that
scribbles over its own map not taking the machine with it, the span
refusing a slice that is not the caller's and a checkpoint refusing to
write over one, and a session that outlived a
.B reset
told there is no such session.
.PP
.B /scripts/osprey-check-wire.py
boots two on one wire and asks for the rest: a key agreed rather than
typed into both, \[*p]p answered in frames of its own, a container
refused to a session that has not said who it is and given to one that
presents a ticket signed by that key, an attribute arriving at the rate
it was asked for, a group whose replies will not fit a frame told so, a container carried across and restarted, a
checkpoint signed by a key the other end does not hold refused with the
container kept where it was, and one too big for a frame refused rather
than half sent.
.PP
.B /scripts/osprey-check-net.py
boots two on one wire as well, gives one machine's interface to a
container, runs
.I netsrv
in it and pings that container's address from the other machine, which
knows nothing about any of it.  It settles two things nothing before it
could: that a frame a container built and handed to the card really
arrives at the other end of the wire, because the other end answers it
-- eight echo requests, eight replies -- and that once the driver's own
two hundred and fifty-six receive buffers have been used up the frames
land in the container's.  It runs twice: once as the kernel is built,
where four hundred pings give
.B "direct 14 copied 396" ,
and once with
.B *ospreypktbuf=256
in
.BR plan9.ini ,
where twelve hundred give
.BR "direct 953 copied 257" .
.PP
.PP
.B /scripts/osprey-check-portable.sh
boots nothing.  It compiles every source in
.B /os/osprey/policy
with
.I 8c
against the 386 headers and with
.I kc
against the riscv64 headers, treating any output at all as a failure.
The container-side library goes with the first of those, against a
thirty-two bit
.B u.h
and an architecture-independent
.BR libc.h ,
which catches an assumption about the width of a type and is not an
Osprey 386 userland: there is not one.  What that settles is
the claim
.B /os/osprey/policy/README
makes and nothing else checked: that the layer depends on nothing of the
machine it was written on.  Everything else in the tree builds it with
.I 6c
against the amd64 headers, where a dependence on a header that exists
only there, or on the width of a type, is invisible.  It catches what a
compiler catches, which is not every width assumption: a pointer cast to
a
.B ulong
compiles on both.
.PP
The three above are sixty-six lines.  Each says
.B ok
or
.B FAIL
a line at a time and keeps its logs when anything failed.  All three want
.B .qemu/9front-root.qcow2
and an ISO built by
.BR /scripts/mkosprey-efi-iso.sh .
.SH WHAT IT COSTS
.PP
Writing
.IP
.nf
\fIid count\fP
.fi
.PP
to
.B bench
puts that many
.B poll
requests on the container's ring one after another, waits for each
answer, and hands back on the same
.B fd
what a round trip cost: the cycles, the nanoseconds and the rate they
were counted at.  The container must have a service process of its own,
or there is nothing to switch to.
.PP
What is measured is a request put on a ring, the process that serves the
container woken and switched to, the request dispatched, the answer put
on the reply ring, and the asking process woken and switched back \-
which is the talk's send, receive and context switch, twice over, in a
Plan 9 kernel rather than a microkernel.  The talk measures 270
nanoseconds.  This measures about 59 microseconds under QEMU without
hardware virtualisation, where the cycle counter is emulated and the
number is not a hardware figure; what it is good for is comparing this
kernel with itself.
.PP
It was 5.5 milliseconds until
.B bench
was written to ask.  Nothing woke a container's service process when a
request was submitted from inside the kernel, so every round trip waited
for the clock to notice.
.PP
A submission through the mapped ring cannot wake anybody \- a process
that never entered the kernel has no way to \- so the clock is what
notices it.  That does not mean it is slow.  A container asking steadily
keeps its service process awake, and then nothing waits for anything:
.IP
.nf
ring osprey0 \fIsize\fP bench 400
.fi
.PP
does four hundred submissions and collections through the mapped ring
in about twenty milliseconds of real time, or fifty microseconds each,
with not one trap taken.
.PP
A request arriving at a service process that has gone to sleep might be
expected to wait for the tick that wakes it, and does not measurably:
twenty separate runs of
.B "bench 1"
\- each against a server that had gone to sleep when the run before it
finished \- take no longer than twenty runs that ask for nothing at all.
Whatever wakes it, it is not waiting a whole tick to be woken.  The 5.5
milliseconds above was the other path, where the asking process slept
too and nothing but the clock could wake either of them.
.SH TICKETS
.PP
A name is only worth what the machine it was said on is worth.  On a
wire, or in a checkpoint that arrived from elsewhere, anyone may say
anything; so a machine that holds a key will sign what is true here, to
be believed there.
.PP
Writing
.IP
.nf
\fIid user seconds\fP
.fi
.PP
to
.B ticket
signs a ticket saying that
.I user
may have container
.I id
for that many seconds \- 0 for as long as the key lasts \- and reading
the same
.B fd
hands it over, once.  Only somebody the container is already for may
ask, and only for a name it is already for: a ticket says a thing that
is already true, so that somewhere else can be told.  It is
.IP
.nf
{echo '7 fred 0' >[1=0]; cat} <>/n/osprey/ticket
.fi
.PP
because the ticket belongs to the
.B fd
that asked for it and to nothing else.  What comes back is
.IP
.nf
user id expiry signature
.fi
.PP
where the signature is HMAC-SHA1 under the machine's key.  A
.B Tauth
of
.IR pip (5)
presents one; see there for what a session does with it.
.PP
.PP
A grant taken back stops a ticket that was made under it: whether the
container is still that name's is asked again at every read, write,
close and stream, not only when the fid was opened, so a
.B revoke
reaches a session that is already at work and a stream already running.
.PP
A checkpoint read from
.B snap
is signed the same way, with a last line
.IP
.nf
sig \fIsignature\fP
.fi
.PP
and a machine with a key will read back only a checkpoint it, or a
machine holding the same key, signed.  This is what makes a migration
between two machines worth anything: the grants a container arrives with
are the grants somebody with the key wrote.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cons (3)
.SH BUGS
.B exec
starts an ELF or a Plan 9 a.out, and what it starts can make only the
calls its buddy maps: open, read, write, close and seek on the buddy's
descriptor table, with 0, 1 and 2 the console; and for a Linux binary
exit, brk, mmap (anonymous, or private of a file), munmap,
rt_sigaction for SIGSEGV with a restorer, rt_sigprocmask, which keeps no
mask, and rt_sigreturn.  A Plan 9 program's declined fault is still a
note the microkernel cannot post.
.B wait
waits for any child, not a named one, and a container may have eight
outstanding.
.B bind
is answered by the policy layer itself, in
.IR dispatch (),
before anything is forwarded: it puts a name in the container's own
table, which is the same table the
.B bind
control message writes and the same one every
.B open
is resolved through.  A container has that table whether it has named a
server or not, so the answer is the same either way.  Naming is
mechanism and not personality \- what a container calls a thing is its
own and travels in its checkpoint, and what the name stands for on this
machine is whatever answers for the container.  Two personalities may
therefore make different things of one global name, and do: a container
that binds a local name to
.B /n/only/proof
opens it through
.I p9srv
and is refused by the kernel's own personality, because
.B /n/only
is a name
.I p9srv
made in its own process.
.B send
and
.B recv
the kernel does answer, in the section above.
.B recv
takes a packet from the queue of a container that has been given an
interface, and is refused by a container that has not.
.B send
puts one on the wire from the other half of the same lending: a
container that has been given an interface also has transmit buffers of
its own in its slice \-
.BR txbuf ,
.B ntxbuf
and
.B txbufsize
in the map \- and
.B send
names one of them and how much of it to send.  Nothing is copied: the
Block that stands for that buffer is queued to the interface and the
card reads the frame out of the container's slice.  The Block comes
back to the container's pool when the card has finished with it, and
until then a
.B send
naming that buffer again is answered
.BR retry ,
so a container cannot write over a frame in flight.  Which buffers may
be written is in
.B txavail
in the map, one bit each, set while that buffer's block is on the free
list: a container finds a buffer with a load out of memory it has
already mapped, and needs no trap and no refusal to do it.  What
.B txavail
holds is worked out from which buffers are in flight and not by walking
the pool's free list: that walk was under the pool's lock at interrupt
level, and a flood of eight hundred frames ended in
.B "ilock ... held by pc"
with the holder and the waiter in the same function.
.I freeb
says which block came back as well as which pool it belongs to, so the
completion needs no lock and no walk at all, and the one walk left \-
unlinking a block a container has asked to send \- is bounded by the
number of blocks there are.  The
semaphore named as
.IR t
to
.B ether
is given when a buffer comes back, so a container with all of them in
flight waits on it in the same
.B select
as everything else.  The source address of a frame is not the
container's to write: the kernel puts the interface's own address in it
as
.I etheroq
does, so a container may say what it likes about where a frame is going
and nothing about where it came from.
.PP
.I p9srv
is a personality for the file operations and nothing else.  A container
that names it has
.BR open ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR stat ,
.B close
and
.B bind
answered outside the kernel; everything else it asks for is answered
.BR error ,
since a server is named for all of what the kernel does not keep.  The
rest of the kernel is untouched by it: the name it opens is resolved by
.IR chan (3)
in the server's process,
.B dev.c
and
.B pgrp.c
are still the kernel's, and every device driver and the whole of the ip
stack are still linked into the kernel and still 9front.  What is real
here is that the path exists and one personality really uses it, not
that any of the kernel has been taken out.
.PP
The right a delegating container gives its server over its own memory is
a capability and is checked as one.  Two containers of glenda's, alike
in everything but that one has delegated to a container
.B none
has been granted, are asked for by a
.I ring
run under
.IR auth/none :
the one that delegated says
.BR "mapped osprey5" ,
the one that did not says
.BR "segattach osprey7: permission denied" .
It is one step and not a chain: a server's own server is not thereby
given what its clients have.
.PP
The other half of the same rule \- that a container's memory is also for
the process bound to it and the processes that one started, which is how
a container reaches its own slice before anybody has been named \- is
shown against the very slice the line above refused.
.B bindproc
binds a process of
.BR none 's
to the container that said
.BR "permission denied" ,
and a
.I ring
that process starts says
.BR "mapped osprey7" .
Same user, same container, same segment: the only difference is which
process is asking.
.PP
.I netsrv
is the other personality, and the one the plan asks for by name: the
protocol stack outside the kernel.  It is the talk's driver task with
all three of its arms \- its own request ring, the semaphore a received
packet gives, and the semaphore a transmit buffer gives when the card
has finished with it \- and what it does between them is arp and icmp
echo, so that a machine on the other end of the wire can ping an address
the kernel has never heard of.  Nothing copies a frame: the card writes
into the container's receive buffers,
.B netsrv
reads the frame where it lies, builds its answer in one of the
container's transmit buffers, and the card reads it out of the same
slice.  What is answered is arp for one address and echo, and nothing
else: there is no routing, no fragmentation, no udp and no tcp, and
nothing above icmp.  It is a demonstration that the path is real and
that the kernel needs to know nothing about it, not a stack anybody
should use.
.PP
.I netsrv
takes the address of the interface it was given from the
.B ea
on the
.B status
line rather than from anywhere it could have been told wrongly, and it
takes a transmit buffer from
.B txavail
in its own map.  Its one trap per frame is the
.B recv
and the
.B send
themselves; the arms, the frame and the answer are all in memory it has
mapped.
.PP
.I p9srv
gives each container it answers for a descriptor group of its own.  The
first request from a container has the server attach that container's
slice and then fork a process for it with
.BR RFFDG ,
so what the server opens for one container is not in another's.  The
process that owns the request ring hands that process the message down a
pipe and takes the answer back the same way; only the message goes
through the pipe, since what it points at is in the span both have
mapped.
.PP
A server keeps a copy of the names of every container it answers for,
read out of
.B status
once rather than once for each
.BR open .
That is not a nicety: with two containers a status read is about a tenth
of a millisecond and with twelve about eight tenths, against a round
trip of about fifty microseconds, so resolving a name that way cost from
two to sixteen times the open it was for, and grew with the number of
containers.  Since
.B bind
is answered before anything is forwarded, a server cannot see a
container's names change, so the kernel tells it: the same message on
the same ring that
.B gone
uses, and the copy is read again.  The check binds a name, reads through
it, binds the same name to a different file and reads again; with the
message ignored it reads the first file both times.
.PP
What the fork does not give, and used to, is a name space.  It was
.B RFNAMEG
as well, and a container's names were a Pgrp: a Plan 9 object no
checkpoint can carry, so a container that migrated arrived having lost
every name it had bound, while the table the policy layer keeps \- which
does cross, and is in
.B status
and in every checkpoint \- resolved nothing at all.  The table is what
resolves a name now, on both paths, and the check migrates a container
to another machine and opens through a name it bound before it left.
.PP
Environments are not in any of this.  Nothing in
.BR os/osprey ,
in either personality or in the library, opens
.B #e
or touches an Egrp; the device is in the kernel for the Plan 9 userland
the kernel still carries, which is the buddy operating system, and no
container reaches it.  A Pgrp is still made for the service process a
container names with
.BR serve ,
and that one is the buddy's: it is the name space the global name a
container's table gives is resolved in, and it is deliberately not the
container's.
.PP
A container that goes is said so to its server.
.B exit
puts a message on the server's request ring, with the container's
identity on it and the operation
.BR gone ,
before anything else about the container is taken apart; there is no
reply, since what the message says is that there is nobody left to reply
to.
.I p9srv
answers it by closing the pipe to that container's process, which ends
it and with it everything it had open, and detaching the slice.  What
the server holds for a container is a process, a pipe and a mapping, and
after
.B gone
it holds none of them; the check counts the processes before and after.
This is the one message that goes the other way along the path a request
takes, and it is the only thing in the request list a container cannot
ask for.
.PP
Scheduling is driven by the clock.  A container with no process bound to
it is billed for whatever its core's run queue says it is running, which
is a policy fiction until
.B bindproc
makes it a real one.
.PP
A container that is waiting says so.  It comes off its core's run queue
while its own process sleeps \- in a
.BR select ,
waiting for a reply, or waiting for room on its ring \- and goes back on
when it is woken, so
.B status
says
.B blocked
rather than
.BR runnable ,
and the scheduler chooses between containers that can run.  Its period
still refills while it waits: a reservation is time the container is
owed and it is owed it whether it is running or not.  The policy layer
has had
.I runtimeblock
and
.I runtimewake
from the start and nothing called either, so until now a container
asleep in a select called itself runnable however long it waited, and a
checkpoint carried a state that was never anything else.
.PP
A packet is charged against a container's network caps while it sits in
its queue and given back when it is taken, and a frame the container
sends is charged while the card has it and given back when the card has
finished, so the caps bound what is in flight rather than what has ever
gone by.  Sending used not to be charged at all.  After twelve hundred
pings each way \- a quarter of a megabyte if nothing were given back \-
a container is left holding a hundred and fifty-six bytes, which is what
the card had at that instant.  Nothing else a container does over the
network is charged unless something writes
.B netcharge
for it.
.PP
.B alloc
charges a container's memory and
.B memfree
gives it back.  Until
.B memfree
was written nothing did:
.B alloc
was half a mechanism, the policy layer's
.I memrelease
was called from nowhere at all, and a container could ask for memory up
to its limit and then never ask again however little of it it was still
using.
.PP
A checkpoint records what a container has used, but what it has used is
counted against a period of a clock the machine it arrives at does not
share.  A container migrated part way through its period arrives with
that much of a budget already spent, and is only itself again at the next
period.
.PP
A message that was in flight goes with a container and so does the data
area, so a request that named a buffer is answered against the buffer it
named.  The data area goes whether a message was in flight or not: it
used to go only when a ring was not empty, so a container that had built
its select arms and was waiting on them lost them, and for no saving,
since a chunk of it that is still zero is not written either way.  The
semaphores go too \- their counts and what has been given and taken \-
which is the one thing the microkernel itself kept for a container that
a checkpoint did not carry, so a container that migrated used to arrive
having lost a wakeup it was owed.
.PP
The container's packets, its pages and the channels the kernel holds for
it do not go: a container that had already opened something comes back
having to open it again, and one whose request to open was still on the
ring has that request answered where it arrives.  Its names do go, and
so open what they name there.
.PP
A checkpoint is one ethernet frame when
.I mig
carries it, and there are two ways to be too big for one.  A container
with a great many messages in flight is the first.  The second is new:
the data area goes whatever the rings hold, so a container that has
written all four kilobytes of its own has a checkpoint of about nine and
a half, where an empty container's is two hundred and thirty-eight bytes
and one that has opened a file and read fifty-five bytes through it is
four hundred and fifty-five.  A chunk that is still zero is not written,
so what it costs is what the container has actually used.  Either way,
.I mig
refuses to send a checkpoint that did not fit rather than sending half
of one, since what it sends it destroys.  Reading
.B snap
refuses for the same reason when every container together does not fit
in the device's buffer, and how much that is has been measured rather
than guessed at.  A container that has written its whole data area is
about nine and a third kilobytes of checkpoint, since the data goes
whatever the rings hold; at the old sixty-four kilobytes the eighth such
container was refused, which is a cliff that arrived with that change
and nothing said so.  The buffer is a hundred and ninety-two kilobytes
now, which is what sixteen of them come to with room over: a checkpoint
of
.B snap
is a checkpoint of the whole machine, so it is sized for the whole
machine.  The check fills every container the machine can hold and
counts them in what comes back.
.PP
A container carries one process.  Two processes billed to one container,
were the device to allow it, could charge it from two cores at once and
lose an update between them.
.PP
The uart is the only driver served by a task.  What its task will do
with a control message is set the line speed and give the uart back;
anything else is answered
.BR error .
It writes what it is given straight out of the device rather than
through the uart's own output staging, so nothing a task sends is
interleaved with what the rest of the kernel sends to the same port.
A uart whose driver has not the four entries a task needs is refused to
a container rather than given one that silently drops what it is told to
send; of the drivers in this kernel the i8250 is the one that has them.
.PP
Giving an interface to a container lends the interface receive buffers
out of the container's own slice, so the card writes packets straight
into memory the container has mapped.  A packet that arrives in a buffer
the driver had queued beforehand is moved into one of those once, so that
what the container is told is always a place in the span; as the driver's
own buffers are used and replaced from the pool this stops happening.
.B status
reports how a container's packets have been arriving.
.PP
Only the interfaces whose driver takes its receive buffers from the
interface's pool can be lent them; of the drivers in this kernel that is
the igbe.  And the lending is slower to take effect than it reads:
igbe's receive ring is two hundred and fifty-six deep and is filled with
the driver's own buffers before the interface is given away, so a
container's buffer put into a ring slot is not used until the card's
head comes round to it again.  Every packet before that is copied.  With
five packets sent to a container that had claimed an interface,
.B status
reported
.B "direct 0 copied 5" .
Four hundred pings later it reported
.B "direct 14 copied 396" :
the head does come round, and the path does fire, but fourteen of four
hundred and ten is not the ordinary case at that buffer count.  A
container lending sixteen receive buffers against a ring two hundred and
fifty-six slots deep, and getting one back per
.B recv
\- the one
.B recv
before it was holding \- can have no more than sixteen of those slots
holding a buffer of its own.  How many it lends is
.B *ospreypktbuf
in
.BR plan9.ini ,
and with it set to the ring's own depth the same twelve hundred pings
give
.B "direct 953 copied 257" :
the copying is the ring's first fill, the driver's own buffers used once
each, and everything after it arrives where the container can read it
without anything moving it there.  The address of the
interface a container was given is on the same line, as
.BR ea ,
since a container that builds an arp body needs to put its own address
in it and has no other way to learn it.  And a card writing into memory a process has mapped is a card
writing where that process may write too, which a container that is not
trusted with its own packets should not be given.
.PP
A key is agreed with whoever answers, not with whoever was meant.
.B pair
says nothing about who is on the other end; the fingerprints in
.B status
are for a person to compare, and until somebody has compared them the key
may have been agreed with anybody who was on the wire.  Nothing repeats
the exchange, so a key lasts until it is changed, and changing it makes
every ticket and every checkpoint written under the old one worthless.
.PP
Three control messages looked a container up without asking who was
writing, where every other one that names a container asks:
.BR poke ,
which writes into a container's data area,
.BR detach ,
which takes it off the core it was attached to, and the second argument
of
.BR delegate ,
which is the container whose ring the requests will go on and which is
thereby given the right to reach the asking container's slice.  All
three ask now, and
.B delegate
wants the server to be a container the caller may have \- its own, or
one whose owner has granted them.
.PP
None of that was reachable.
.B ctl
is mode 0664 and belongs to whoever booted the machine, so another user
cannot write a control message at all; what a grant buys them is a
segment and a
.IR pip (5)
session, which is where the checks that were already there do the work.
The three are defence in depth against a
.B ctl
that is opened up later, and opening it up is not as simple as changing
the mode: the commands that name no container \-
.BR key ,
.BR pair ,
.BR balance ,
.B schedule
\- have nothing to check and should not be anybody's.  For the same
reason none of the three has a line in the check: a check would have to
write a control message as another user, and another user cannot.
.PP
.I ospreywrite
is the device's whole control-message switch and was nine hundred and
sixty-eight lines;
.B ether
and
.B uart
are functions of their own now and the next control message worth more
than a few lines should be one too.  What prompted that was a compiler
that died on the function, and the compiler was wrong rather than the
function too big:
.B NVAR
in
.B /utils/cc/cc.h
counted variables in units of
.IR sizeof(ulong) ,
where
.B bits.c
addresses them in lanes of thirty-two, so on a host with a sixty-four
bit
.B ulong
the optimiser handed out numbers it could not address and wrote past a
.B Bits
on the stack.  It is fixed, and so is a second of the same kind found by reading for
it: the lexer built a name or a number in
.BR symb ,
a fixed global, without the bound the macro processor beside it has
always had, so a four thousand character identifier wrote past the end
of it and
.I 6c
exited zero and wrote an object.
.B /scripts/cc-check.sh
is the check, over all nine compilers, and it covers a third of the same
kind:
.I mpatof
rounded a decimal constant three times where once is wanted, so
better than a fifth of decimal literals came out one unit in the last
place from the double nearest them.  It had not been spoiling objects:
.I ospreywrite
is the only function in this kernel whose variables the optimiser cannot
all track, and building
.B devosprey.c
with the compiler before the fix and after it gives the same object byte
for byte.  What the bug corrupted was the compiler's own stack, which
hit padding until one more local moved things.
.PP
An interrupt giving a semaphore takes the lock that guards them, and
every packet that arrives gives one, so nothing that formats or parses
is done while holding it:
.B status
and a checkpoint copy the counts out under it and print them after
letting it go, and a checkpoint being read takes its line apart first
and stores the numbers after.  That is why
.B *ospreysem
has a ceiling of sixty-four where the other four have only the arena's:
the copy has to go somewhere fixed.
.PP
A container may write every byte of its own map, so nothing in the
kernel believes what it finds there:
.I mapfix
puts the geometry back from the kernel's own constants before any of it
is read, and every reader in
.B /os/osprey/adapter/ospreyrt.c
calls it.  Two did not until lately, and
.B status
prints what one of them returns \- so a container could scribble a false
data offset into its map and have the kernel repeat it as its own
account of where that container's data area is, which is the one thing a
server reads
.B status
to avoid.
.PP
A ring carries one producer and one consumer.  Two processes submitting
to the same container, or a process submitting while another writes
.B submit
to
.B ctl
for it, will corrupt the ring; the device sleeps one waiter per container
per direction and does not arbitrate between more.
